program            = 005_va_list_forwarding
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Variadic argument list forwarded by value to v-style consumers, including a two-level outer to middle to innermost chain, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Header exception: this program includes <stdarg.h>. That is the one sanctioned header
exception for area 07, because variadic functions cannot be written at all without
va_list, va_start, va_arg, va_end and va_copy, and because the v-style consumers here
declare a va_list parameter, which cannot be spelled without the header at all. stdarg.h
is in bcc's own bundled freestanding set (docs/technical-specifications.md line 208) and
is also a freestanding header supplied by the reference compiler, so it compiles
identically under both oracles and the program remains a single-file reproducer. No other
header is included; in particular stdio.h is not included, because bcc ships none. printf
is hand-declared.
Warning gate: this program passes the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror unchanged, which was verified by running
that gate over this program on the reference compiler. Area 07 sanctions no deviation, and
this record therefore carries no ub_audit_flags key: the record layer expresses "no
deviation" by omitting the key rather than by restating the gate, because a value naming
the whole gate would be declaring a deviation that deviates in nothing. The sanitizer half
of the audit was run too - a native build with -fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all executes to completion with no diagnostic - so the
undefined-behaviour-freedom argument below is machine-checked as well as written down.
The sequencing argument for va_list forwarding, which is the central correctness rule of
this program. C11 provides that the object ap may be passed as an argument to another
function, and that if that function invokes the va_arg macro with parameter ap then the
value of ap in the calling function is indeterminate and shall be passed to the va_end
macro prior to any further reference to ap. This program obeys that rule exactly, as
follows. Each of the three variadic wrappers calls va_start on its own list, naming the
last named parameter, which is count in fwd_ints and fwd_doubles and n_dbl in fwd_mixed.
Each wrapper then passes that list by value to a v-style callee, assigns the callee's
return value to a named local, and calls va_end on the list. No wrapper calls va_arg at
any point, either before or after forwarding, so there are exactly zero va_arg
invocations in the three wrappers and the caller's indeterminate list is never read. The
va_end call after forwarding is not merely permitted but required, and it is the only
operation performed on the list after the callee returns.
The v-style consumers read the forwarded list with va_arg only. None of them calls
va_start, va_copy or va_end, because C11 requires va_end to be invoked by the same
function that invoked va_start or va_copy for that list, and a consumer neither creates
nor destroys the list. No consumer assigns to its va_list parameter and none takes its
address.
Two-level forwarding is exercised deliberately. fwd_doubles forwards its list to
vmid_doubles, which forwards the same list by value a second time to vsum_doubles, which
is the only function in that chain that calls va_arg. After vsum_doubles returns, the
list is indeterminate in vmid_doubles, which does nothing further with it and simply
returns, and it is indeterminate in fwd_doubles, which calls va_end and nothing else.
This is the conforming way to build a chain of v-style helpers.
No list is ever read past the number of arguments actually supplied at its call site.
Each consumer retrieves exactly as many arguments as its count parameters specify, and
each call site supplies exactly that many, so there is no over-read.
Retrieval types: int and double arguments are not subject to the default argument
promotions, so va_arg(ap, int) and va_arg(ap, double) retrieve exactly the types
supplied. The floating arguments are written as double literals with no f suffix, so no
promotion is involved and va_arg(ap, float) never appears.
Arithmetic: no signed overflow is possible. The integer totals are 9, 21, -1 and -1, all
far inside the 32-bit int range, and floating addition is on dyadic rationals only. No int
is ever added to a double accumulator or the reverse. There is no shift, no division, no
aliasing violation, no read of uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between
sequence points, and no dependence on padding or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
Evaluation order: main stores each wrapper's return value in a named temporary before
printing it, so no call has more than one side-effecting argument. In the runtime variants
each volatile object is read by a single simple assignment into a plain local of the same
type before the call, so the arguments in the call expression are pure reads of plain
locals and the unspecified argument-evaluation order is unobservable.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Why va_list forwarding is a genuine cross-backend test rather than a portability hazard.
The representation of va_list differs by target. On x86-64 System V it is an array type
whose element describes a register save area plus an overflow area, so passing it by value
decays to passing a pointer and the callee's va_arg mutates the caller's state. On i686
cdecl every argument is pushed on the stack and va_list is effectively a plain pointer
passed genuinely by value, so the callee's va_arg does not mutate the caller's state.
AArch64 AAPCS64 and RISC-V 64 LP64D each use their own register save area layouts. The
relevant ABI facts are recorded at docs/technical-specifications.md lines 546, 553, 559
and 565. This divergence in representation is precisely why the C standard declares the
caller's list indeterminate after forwarding, and because this program never reads a
forwarded list again it is fully conforming on all four targets and must produce identical
output on all four. Any cross-backend difference here is therefore a genuine finding
rather than a documented implementation-defined difference.
Width normalization: the types long and unsigned long are never used, because sizeof(long)
and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64
(docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457 to 462). Only int and double appear, and the
printf conversions are limited to %s, %d and %.6f. No address, no pointer value, no
sizeof result and no plain char value is printed, and nothing derived from a va_list
object itself is printed.
Floating determinism: every floating literal is a dyadic rational exactly representable in
IEEE binary64 (1.5, 2.25, 4.125, 0.25, 8.5, 3.0625, 0.125, 2.5), and all floating output
is printed at fixed precision with %.6f, so the rendered text is byte-identical on all
four backends.
Output ordering in the mixed cases: the v-style consumer prints its integer lines, then
its floating lines, then its own floating sum line, and main prints the integer sum line
after the wrapper returns. The golden record reflects that order exactly.
Cross-backend comparability: integer and double variadic argument passing was measured to
produce byte-identical results on all four targets at all three optimization levels, so no
target is restricted and all three oracles are enabled.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
